--- page 10 ----------------------------------------------------
Chart 8: Distribution of Population by Place of Birth
Regarding the local population, 49.5% (281,381) of them were born in Macao, an increase of 2.9
percentage points from 46.6% in 2011, and those born in mainland China accounted for 42.5% (241,909).
Analysed by age, 69.5% of the local population aged below 40 were born in Macao, whereas the majority of
those aged 40 and above were born in mainland China, at 61.9%.
1.4  Nationality and Ethnicity
In terms of nationality, 89.2% (608,379) of the total population were of Chinese nationality, down by 3.1
p
ercentage points from 2011. The population of Portuguese nationality increased by 3,971 from ten years
ago to 8,991, and its proportion in the total population rose by 0.4 percentage points to 1.3%. In the light of
economic development, there was an increase in the number of people coming from other places to work in
Macao compared to 2011, and the composition of the population became more diverse. The proportion of
population of other nationalities (64,700) grew by 2.7 percentage points to 9.5%; Filipinos and Vietnamese
comprised 5.0% and 1.8% of the total population respectively.
Analysed by ethnicity, the population of Chinese ethnicity totalled 609,863, taking up 89.4% of the total

--- page 16 ----------------------------------------------------
groups recorded a rise, with the rate for the elderly population (aged 65 and above) rising markedly by 17.4
percentage points over 2011 to 89.9%.
Meanwhile, the proportion of those aged 15 and above who cannot read or write was just 1.9%, a drop
of 2.0 percentage points from 2011. The corresponding share for females was 2.6%, higher than that for males
(1.0%); yet, the gap between them narrowed compared to 2011.
1.7  Language Skills
 Of the population aged 3 and above, 81.0% used Cantonese as their usual language, a fall of 2.3
perce
ntage points over 2011; 5.4% used other Chinese dialects and 4.7% used Mandarin, both down by 0.3
percentage points. With an increase in incoming migrants from other countries/territories, the proportion of
those who used English as their usual language expanded notably by 1.3 percentage points from ten years
ago to 3.6%.
Regarding the overall language skills, 86.2% of the population were fluent in Cantonese, down by 3.8
percentage points from 2011; those who were fluent in Mandarin and English took up 45.0% and 22.7%
respectively, up by 3.6 percentage points and 1.6 percentage points. Besides, the proportion of those who
spoke fluent Portuguese nudged down by 0.1 percentage point to 2.3%.
